Optional, but when the code exists but is expired, it returns the expired coupon. The applyCoupon service then distinguishes between these cases—nonexistent codes get \"Invalid coupon code\" while expired ones get \"Coupon expired\". My stub handles this by returning empty for unknown codes like \"NOPE\" and returning the expired coupon for codes marked as expired.\n\nFor the InventoryService, it's an interface that the TypeConverter stubs out using a factory method that takes a stock map and returns an implementation looking up values from that map. I think I have enough of the design now to start writing the actual code.\n\nOne concern: the `buildCartWithCoupon` helper in the test calls `couponStore.findCoupon()` to retrieve the current coupon, which means the test setup depends on the coupon store structure—this could get confusing if the store format becomes more complex.\n\nI'm realizing there's an issue with my `shouldApplyCoupon` test signature. The parameters include `String currentCouponCode`, but when that cell is blank in the test table, it becomes `null`, which creates ambiguity about what that means.\n\nFor the test body, I'll build the cart manually and set the current coupon only if it's non-null—this keeps things straightforward since JUnit handles String parameters natively without needing a TypeConverter. I should also note that I'm assuming Cart has a factory method like `Cart.of(Map<String, Integer>)` that I'll be using directly in the test.\n\nNow I'm reviewing how the CouponStore TypeConverter parses the format `[SAVE10: 10%, SAVE5: $5.00]`—splitting by comma and whitespace gives individual entries, then each entry splits by colon to separate the code from the discount value.\n\nParsing product-specific coupons... Checking if the percentage sign needs quoting in the table syntax — the skill notes say values are quoted when they contain `|`, start with `[`, or start with `{`, so `%` doesn't require special handling. Since `10%` is nested inside the coupon array value itself, it should be fine as-is.\n\nNow I'm realizing the real issue: TableTest interprets `[SAVE10: 10%]` as a map literal, but my parameter type is `CouponStore`, not `Map`, so TableTest needs to know how to convert that map syntax into a `CouponStore` object. `toCouponStore` TypeConverter.\n\nHmm, but does TableTest pass the entire string `[SAVE10: 10%]` to the TypeConverter, or does it try to parse it as a collection first?\n\nLooking at the TableTest rules: TypeConverter methods handle custom types. If the parameter is of a custom type (`CouponStore`), TableTest should pass the raw cell value string to the TypeConverter. So `[SAVE10: 10%]` would be passed as the string `\"[SAVE10: 10%]\"` to `toCouponStore`.\n\nActually, I'm not 100% sure about this. TableTest might first check if the value looks like a collection and try to parse it. But since `CouponStore` is not a collection type, it should use the TypeConverter.\n\nLet me check if the `[` at the start of the value would cause issues. The quoting rule says \"Starts with `[`\" — quote to avoid list syntax. Since my column value IS `[SAVE10: 10%]`, it starts with `[`, so TableTest might try to parse it as a list first, which would fail. To be safe, I should quote the values in the table so they're treated as raw strings passed to the TypeConverter.\n\nSo I'll quote all the problematic values: `CouponStore` values, `ProductCatalogue` values, `Cart` values, and `InventoryService` values — anything that starts with `[` or `{` or contains `|`.\n\nThe TypeConverter will receive these as quoted strings and my parsing logic already handles stripping the outer brackets, so `\"[WIDGET: 10.00, GADGET: 5.00]\"` becomes the map I need. Even empty values like `\"[]\"` should be quoted to avoid being interpreted as an actual empty list. to extract the inner content, then splits by `,` to parse the collection. So I need to quote any cell value that starts with `[` or `{`. For the other columns like Coupon, simple values like `10%`, `$5.00`, and `WIDGET:$3.00` don't need quoting. But for ProductCatalogue and Cart, those map-like and list-like structures need the quotes. Let me verify the Inventory column values too.\n\nActually, I'm realizing I might be overthinking this. Looking back at the examples, map-type columns like `[Alice: 95, Bob: 87]` aren't quoted when the parameter type is directly `Map<String, Integer>`. But when I'm using a custom type with a TypeConverter, TableTest needs the raw string representation, so quoting prevents it from trying to parse the collection syntax itself. Let me reconsider the table design to make sure I'm handling this correctly.\n\nFor the `shouldApplyCoupon` table, the `Current Coupon` and `Active Coupon After?` columns are just plain strings—no quoting needed there. But I need to check if the `Coupon Store` column values like `\"[SAVE10: 10%]\"` are being quoted correctly...\n\nActually, when I write that in the table, the outer quotes are the table's quoting mechanism, so my TypeConverter receives the raw string `[SAVE10: 10%]` and parses it step by step: strips the brackets, splits by comma to get one entry, then splits that entry by the colon pattern to extract the coupon code and percentage value.\n\nFor the multi-coupon case with `[SAVE10: 10%, SAVE5: $5.00]`, splitting by comma gives me two entries, and I'm already using `entry.trim()` after splitting to handle any leading whitespace, so the space before `SAVE5` gets cleaned up properly. 5.00 is a dollar amount, so it becomes `Coupon.productOff(\"WIDGET\", new BigDecimal(\"5.00\"))`. Let me verify this works for the store entry too—when I parse `WIDGET5: WIDGET:$5.00`, I split the outer definition first to get the key and value, then parse the value which contains the product-specific coupon format. So the regex pattern `:\\$` correctly matches the literal `:$` sequence, which means splitting `\"WIDGET:$5.00\"` by that pattern gives me `[\"WIDGET\", \"5.00\"]` as expected. Now I'm ready to write out the clean Java implementation, but first I need to clarify which `@TypeConverter` annotation package to use and whether I actually need custom type conversion logic for this problem.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3733,"cache_read_input_tokens":36837,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3733,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4c3bb6d-ab68-4398-8b75-0c630070e2f4","uuid":"590af0b0-acaf-4a1f-8aee-06e9571074d8"}
