{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QoDNDvXrDNXb2VxYzWLkku","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Custom Type Converter Methods\n     2→\n     3→## Table of Contents\n     4→- [Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter](#prefer-built-in-conversion-before-writing-a-converter)\n     5→- [Writing Custom Converter Methods](#writing-custom-converter-methods) (Java / Kotlin)\n     6→- [Using @TypeConverterSources](#using-typeconvertersources)\n     7→- [Converter Method Requirements](#converter-method-requirements)\n     8→- [Search Strategy](#search-strategy)\n     9→- [Handling Null Values](#handling-null-values)\n    10→- [Domain-Specific Formatting](#domain-specific-formatting)\n    11→- [Converter Methods with Defaults](#converter-methods-with-defaults)\n    12→- [Common Wrapper Types](#common-wrapper-types)\n    13→\n    14→## Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter\n    15→\n    16→JUnit can convert strings to `Class<?>` when the value is a fully-qualified class name. Write `java.lang.RuntimeException` in the table instead of `RuntimeException` plus a custom `@TypeConverter`. This also works for collection elements: `[java.lang.RuntimeException, java.lang.NullPointerException]` → `List<Class<?>>`.\n    17→\n    18→Only write a converter method when built-in conversion does not cover the type.\n    19→\n    20→### Non-obvious built-in conversions\n    21→\n    22→The common types (primitives, `String`, `Path`, `URI`, `URL`, `UUID`, `LocalDate`, `LocalTime`, `LocalDateTime`) are supported as expected. Less obvious conversions worth knowing:\n    23→\n    24→| Table value | Parameter type | Notes |\n    25→|---|---|---|\n    26→| `\"SECONDS\"` | `TimeUnit` (any enum) | Enum name, case-sensitive |\n    27→| `\"0xF\"`, `\"017\"` | `int`/`long` (and boxed) | Hex and octal literals work |\n    28→| `\"java.lang.Integer\"` | `Class<?>` | Fully-qualified name required |\n    29→| `\"java.lang.Thread$State\"` | `Class<?>` | `$` for nested classes |\n    30→| `\"byte\"` | `Class<?>` | Primitive type names work |\n    31→| `\"char[]\"` | `Class<?>` | Array type names work |\n    32→| `\"PT3S\"`, `\"PT1H30M\"` | `Duration` | ISO 8601 duration format |\n    33→| `\"P2M6D\"` | `Period` | ISO 8601 period format |\n    34→| `\"JPY\"` | `Currency` | ISO 4217 currency code |\n    35→| `\"en-US\"` | `Locale` | IETF BCP 47 language tag |\n    36→\n    37→**Enums**: Write the enum constant name — `\"SECONDS\"` not `\"TimeUnit.SECONDS\"`. The parameter type tells JUnit which enum to use.\n    38→\n    39→```java\n    40→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    41→    Scenario       | Unit    | Duration?\n    42→    In seconds     | SECONDS | PT3S\n    43→    In minutes     | MINUTES | PT5M\n    44→    \"\"\")\n    45→void formats_duration(TimeUnit unit, Duration duration) { ... }\n    46→```\n    47→\n    48→---\n    49→\n    50→## Writing Custom Converter Methods\n    51→\n    52→When JUnit's built-in converters don't support your parameter type, add custom type converter methods annotated with `@TypeConverter`.\n    53→\n    54→## Java\n    55→\n    56→Place custom converter methods as `@TypeConverter`-annotated `public static` methods in **a public test class** or a class listed in `@TypeConverterSources`.\n    57→\n    58→**IMPORTANT:** The test class must be declared `public` for TableTest to discover converter methods:\n    59→```java\n    60→public class MyTest {  // Must be public, not package-private\n    61→    // Converter methods here will be found\n    62→}\n    63→```\n    64→\n    65→```java\n    66→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    67→    Date       | Days Until?\n    68→    today      | 0\n    69→    tomorrow   | 1\n    70→    \"\"\")\n    71→void testDaysUntil(LocalDate date, int expected) {\n    72→    assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date));\n    73→}\n    74→\n    75→@TypeConverter\n    76→public static LocalDate parseLocalDate(String input) {\n    77→    return switch (input) {\n    78→        case \"today\" -> LocalDate.now();\n    79→        case \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1);\n    80→        default -> LocalDate.parse(input);\n    81→    };\n    82→}\n    83→```\n    84→\n    85→## Kotlin\n    86→\n    87→For Kotlin tests, there are two ways to declare converter methods:\n    88→\n    89→### Option 1: Package-Level Functions\n    90→\n    91→Declare converter methods at package level in the same file as the test class:\n    92→\n    93→```kotlin\n    94→// At package level (top of file, outside class)\n    95→@TypeConverter\n    96→fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n    97→    \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n    98→    \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n    99→    else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n   100→}\n   101→\n   102→class DateTest {\n   103→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   104→        Date       | Days Until?\n   105→        today      | 0\n   106→        tomorrow   | 1\n   107→        \"\"\")\n   108→    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n   109→        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n   110→    }\n   111→}\n   112→```\n   113→\n   114→### Option 2: Companion Object with @JvmStatic\n   115→\n   116→Declare converter methods in the companion object with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n   117→\n   118→```kotlin\n   119→class DateTest {\n   120→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   121→        Date       | Days Until?\n   122→        today      | 0\n   123→        tomorrow   | 1\n   124→        \"\"\")\n   125→    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n   126→        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n   127→    }\n   128→\n   129→    companion object {\n   130→        @JvmStatic\n   131→        @TypeConverter\n   132→        fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n   133→            \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n   134→            \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n   135→            else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n   136→        }\n   137→    }\n   138→}\n   139→```\n   140→\n   141→**Note**: `@Nested` inner classes in Kotlin cannot have companion objects. Use package-level functions or outer class companion object instead.\n   142→\n   143→## Using @TypeConverterSources\n   144→\n   145→For shared converter methods across multiple test classes:\n   146→\n   147→**Java:**\n   148→```java\n   149→@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters.class)\n   150→class DateTest {\n   151→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   152→        ...\n   153→        \"\"\")\n   154→    void testWithSharedConverters(LocalDate date, Duration duration) { ... }\n   155→}\n   156→```\n   157→\n   158→**Kotlin** — use an `object` declaration with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n   159→```kotlin\n   160→object DateConverters {\n   161→    @JvmStatic\n   162→    @TypeConverter\n   163→    fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n   164→        \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n   165→        \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n   166→        else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n   167→    }\n   168→}\n   169→\n   170→@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters::class)\n   171→class DateTest {\n   172→    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n   173→        ...\n   174→        \"\"\")\n   175→    fun testWithSharedConverters(date: LocalDate, duration: Duration) { ... }\n   176→}\n   177→```\n   178→\n   179→## Converter Method Requirements\n   180→\n   181→A converter method will be used when it:\n   182→1. Is annotated with `@TypeConverter`\n   183→2. Is defined as a `public static` method in a `public class`\n   184→3. Accepts exactly one parameter\n   185→4. Returns an object of the target parameter type\n   186→5. **Is the only `@TypeConverter` method matching the above criteria in the class**\n   187→\n   188→There is no specific naming pattern required, but `parse<TypeName>` (e.g., `parseLocalDate`, `parseMoney`) is conventional.\n   189→\n   190→### One Converter Method Per Target Type\n   191→\n   192→You cannot have multiple converter methods with the same target type (same return type), even with different names:\n   193→\n   194→```java\n   195→// WRONG - TableTest cannot determine which to use\n   196→@TypeConverter\n   197→public static Map<String, String> parseRequest(String value) { ... }\n   198→@TypeConverter\n   199→public static Map<String, String> parseResponse(String value) { ... }\n   200→\n   201→// CORRECT - Single converter handles both formats\n   202→@TypeConverter\n   203→public static Map<String, String> parseRequestOrResponse(String value) {\n   204→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   205→        // Handle response format\n   206→    } else {\n   207→        // Handle request format\n   208→    }\n   209→    return ...;\n   210→}\n   211→```\n   212→\n   213→**Even better - split into separate columns instead:**\n   214→```java\n   215→// BEST - No converter method needed\n   216→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   217→    Request Status | Request ms | Response Status? | Response ms?\n   218→    OK             | 10         | OK               | <50\n   219→    \"\"\")\n   220→void test(String reqStatus, Integer reqMs, String respStatus, Long respMs) {\n   221→    // Use parseResponseTime converter for respMs to handle \"<50\" format\n   222→}\n   223→\n   224→@TypeConverter\n   225→public static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n   226→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   227→        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n   228→    }\n   229→    return Long.parseLong(value);\n   230→}\n   231→```\n   232→\n   233→## Search Strategy\n   234→\n   235→TableTest searches for converter methods in this order (stops at first match):\n   236→\n   237→**Java:**\n   238→1. Current test class (including inherited methods)\n   239→2. Enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests, starting with direct outer class)\n   240→3. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n   241→4. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n   242→\n   243→**Kotlin:**\n   244→1. Current file (package-level functions and outer class companion object)\n   245→2. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n   246→3. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n   247→\n   248→If a converter method isn't being found, check that it meets the requirements above and is in a location that matches the search order.\n   249→\n   250→## Handling Null Values\n   251→\n   252→When table cells can be blank (representing null), use appropriate return types:\n   253→\n   254→### Primitive vs Boxed Types\n   255→\n   256→```java\n   257→// WRONG - primitives cannot be null\n   258→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   259→    Value | Time\n   260→    OK    | 10\n   261→          |        <- Blank cell causes error: \"null cannot be assigned to primitive type long\"\n   262→    \"\"\")\n   263→void test(String value, long time) { ... }\n   264→\n   265→// CORRECT - use boxed type\n   266→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   267→    Value | Time\n   268→    OK    | 10\n   269→          |        <- Blank cell becomes null\n   270→    \"\"\")\n   271→void test(String value, Long time) { ... }\n   272→```\n   273→\n   274→### Converter Methods with Null Input\n   275→\n   276→Converter methods receive `null` for blank cells. Handle this appropriately:\n   277→\n   278→```java\n   279→// Return null for blank input\n   280→@TypeConverter\n   281→public static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n   282→    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n   283→        return null;  // Blank cell -> null value\n   284→    }\n   285→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   286→        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n   287→    }\n   288→    return Long.parseLong(value);\n   289→}\n   290→\n   291→// Return empty collection for blank input\n   292→@TypeConverter\n   293→public static List<String> parseItems(String value) {\n   294→    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n   295→        return List.of();  // Blank cell -> empty list\n   296→    }\n   297→    return Arrays.asList(value.split(\",\"));\n   298→}\n   299→```\n   300→\n   301→**Common pattern:** Blank cells in the table map to null/empty in the domain.\n   302→\n   303→## Domain-Specific Formatting\n   304→\n   305→Converter methods enable domain-specific formatting conventions that improve table readability:\n   306→\n   307→```java\n   308→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   309→    Scenario     | Response Time?\n   310→    Fast         | <50\n   311→    Acceptable   | <150\n   312→    Slow         | <500\n   313→    \"\"\")\n   314→void testResponseTime(Long maxResponseTimeMs) {\n   315→    // maxResponseTimeMs is 50, 150, 500 respectively\n   316→}\n   317→\n   318→@TypeConverter\n   319→public static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n   320→    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n   321→        return null;\n   322→    }\n   323→    // Handle \"less than\" notation\n   324→    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n   325→        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n   326→    }\n   327→    return Long.parseLong(value);\n   328→}\n   329→```\n   330→\n   331→**Benefits:**\n   332→- `<50` clearly communicates \"less than 50ms\" in the table\n   333→- Converter handles the formatting detail\n   334→- Table is self-documenting without code comments\n   335→\n   336→**Other examples:**\n   337→- Duration: `5m`, `30s` -> milliseconds\n   338→- Currency: `$100`, `EUR50` -> numeric values\n   339→- Percentage: `50%` -> 0.5\n   340→- File size: `10KB`, `5MB` -> bytes\n   341→\n   342→## Converter Methods with Defaults\n   343→\n   344→Converter methods can provide sensible defaults for missing map entries, making tables clearer when properties vary by scenario:\n   345→\n   346→```java\n   347→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   348→    Scenario          | Config                                  | Expected?\n   349→    Basic request     | [method: GET]                           | OK\n   350→    With timeout      | [method: POST, timeout: 5000]           | OK\n   351→    Full config       | [method: POST, timeout: 5000, retry: 3] | OK\n   352→    \"\"\")\n   353→void testRequest(Map<String, String> config, String expected) {\n   354→    RequestConfig request = buildRequestConfig(config);\n   355→    assertEquals(expected, process(request));\n   356→}\n   357→\n   358→@TypeConverter\n   359→public static RequestConfig buildRequestConfig(Map<String, String> config) {\n   360→    if (config == null || config.isEmpty()) {\n   361→        return RequestConfig.defaults();\n   362→    }\n   363→    return new RequestConfig(\n   364→        config.getOrDefault(\"method\", \"GET\"),           // Default: GET\n   365→        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"timeout\", \"3000\")), // Default: 3000ms\n   366→        config.get(\"auth\"),                             // Default: null (no auth)\n   367→        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"retry\", \"1\")),     // Default: 1 retry\n   368→        parseBoolean(config.getOrDefault(\"cache\", \"false\")) // Default: no cache\n   369→    );\n   370→}\n   371→```\n   372→\n   373→**Benefits:**\n   374→- Each scenario only specifies what varies from defaults\n   375→- Table focuses on the interesting variations\n   376→- Defaults are documented in one place (converter method)\n   377→- No sea of blank columns\n   378→\n   379→**When to use:**\n   380→- Different scenarios need different subsets of properties\n   381→- Most properties have sensible default values\n   382→- Alternative is many blank cells in separate columns\n   383→\n   384→**When not to use:**\n   385→- All scenarios use the same properties (use separate columns instead)\n   386→- No sensible defaults exist (every property is required)\n   387→\n   388→## Common Wrapper Types\n   389→\n   390→JUnit's built-in converters don't handle wrapper types like `Optional`, `Result`, or `Either`. Converter methods are needed for these common patterns.\n   391→\n   392→### Optional<T>\n   393→\n   394→```java\n   395→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   396→    Scenario        | Feature Toggles      | Result?\n   397→    Feature enabled | [feature-x: true]    | true\n   398→    Not registered  | [:]                  | empty\n   399→    Feature disabled| [feature-x: false]   | false\n   400→    \"\"\")\n   401→void testFeatureToggle(Map<String, Boolean> toggles, Optional<Boolean> result) {\n   402→    assertEquals(result, featureResolver.find(\"feature-x\", toggles));\n   403→}\n   404→\n   405→@TypeConverter\n   406→public static Optional<Boolean> result(String value) {\n   407→    return switch (value) {\n   408→        case \"empty\" -> Optional.empty();\n   409→        case \"true\" -> Optional.of(true);\n   410→        case \"false\" -> Optional.of(false);\n   411→        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n   412→    };\n   413→}\n   414→```\n   415→\n   416→**Pattern**: Use simple string representations (`empty`, `true`, `false`) rather than trying to parse `Optional.empty()` syntax.\n   417→\n   418→### Optional<String>\n   419→\n   420→```java\n   421→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   422→    Scenario     | Input  | Result?\n   423→    Found        | active | active\n   424→    Not found    | unused | empty\n   425→    \"\"\")\n   426→void testLookup(String input, Optional<String> result) {\n   427→    assertEquals(result, lookup(input));\n   428→}\n   429→\n   430→@TypeConverter\n   431→public static Optional<String> result(String value) {\n   432→    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n   433→}\n   434→```\n   435→\n   436→### Result<T, E> (or Either<L, R>)\n   437→\n   438→For Result/Either types, use string notation to indicate success vs failure:\n   439→\n   440→```java\n   441→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   442→    Scenario       | Input | Result?\n   443→    Valid input    | 42    | OK\n   444→    Invalid input  | abc   | ERROR: Not a number\n   445→    Out of range   | -1    | ERROR: Must be positive\n   446→    \"\"\")\n   447→void testValidation(String input, Result<Integer, String> result) {\n   448→    assertEquals(result, validator.validate(input));\n   449→}\n   450→\n   451→@TypeConverter\n   452→public static Result<Integer, String> result(String value) {\n   453→    if (value.equals(\"OK\")) {\n   454→        return Result.ok(42);  // Use value from Input column\n   455→    }\n   456→    if (value.startsWith(\"ERROR: \")) {\n   457→        return Result.error(value.substring(7));\n   458→    }\n   459→    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n   460→}\n   461→```\n   462→\n   463→**Pattern**: Prefix error cases with `ERROR:` to distinguish from success cases.\n   464→\n   465→**Better approach**: Use separate columns for success value and error message:\n   466→\n   467→```java\n   468→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   469→    Scenario       | Input | Success? | Error?\n   470→    Valid input    | 42    | 42       |\n   471→    Invalid input  | abc   |          | Not a number\n   472→    Out of range   | -1    |          | Must be positive\n   473→    \"\"\")\n   474→void testValidation(String input, Integer success, String error) {\n   475→    Result<Integer, String> expected = success != null\n   476→        ? Result.ok(success)\n   477→        : Result.error(error);\n   478→    assertEquals(expected, validator.validate(input));\n   479→}\n   480→```\n   481→\n   482→This avoids the converter method entirely and makes the table clearer.\n   483→\n   484→### Multiple Optional Parameters\n   485→\n   486→When you have multiple `Optional` parameters in the same test, use different column naming:\n   487→\n   488→```java\n   489→@TableTest(\"\"\"\n   490→    Scenario          | Primary?  | Fallback?\n   491→    Both present      | primary   | fallback\n   492→    Primary only      | primary   | empty\n   493→    Fallback only     | empty     | fallback\n   494→    Neither present   | empty     | empty\n   495→    \"\"\")\n   496→void testOptionals(Optional<String> primary, Optional<String> fallback) {\n   497→    // Test with both optionals\n   498→}\n   499→\n   500→@TypeConverter\n   501→public static Optional<String> primary(String value) {\n   502→    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n   503→}\n   504→\n   505→@TypeConverter\n   506→public static Optional<String> fallback(String value) {\n   507→    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n   508→}\n   509→```\n   510→\n   511→**Important**: Column name matching works here. The converter method name should match the parameter name, and TableTest will use the appropriate converter for each column.\n   512→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4c3bb6d-ab68-4398-8b75-0c630070e2f4","uuid":"237c644a-fac9-4dcf-a0f6-d975e70499d7","timestamp":"2026-04-06T21:01:30.358Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775508642173/skills/tabletest/references/type-converters.md","content":"# Custom Type Converter Methods\n\n## Table of Contents\n- [Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter](#prefer-built-in-conversion-before-writing-a-converter)\n- [Writing Custom Converter Methods](#writing-custom-converter-methods) (Java / Kotlin)\n- [Using @TypeConverterSources](#using-typeconvertersources)\n- [Converter Method Requirements](#converter-method-requirements)\n- [Search Strategy](#search-strategy)\n- [Handling Null Values](#handling-null-values)\n- [Domain-Specific Formatting](#domain-specific-formatting)\n- [Converter Methods with Defaults](#converter-methods-with-defaults)\n- [Common Wrapper Types](#common-wrapper-types)\n\n## Prefer Built-in Conversion Before Writing a Converter\n\nJUnit can convert strings to `Class<?>` when the value is a fully-qualified class name. Write `java.lang.RuntimeException` in the table instead of `RuntimeException` plus a custom `@TypeConverter`. This also works for collection elements: `[java.lang.RuntimeException, java.lang.NullPointerException]` → `List<Class<?>>`.\n\nOnly write a converter method when built-in conversion does not cover the type.\n\n### Non-obvious built-in conversions\n\nThe common types (primitives, `String`, `Path`, `URI`, `URL`, `UUID`, `LocalDate`, `LocalTime`, `LocalDateTime`) are supported as expected. Less obvious conversions worth knowing:\n\n| Table value | Parameter type | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `\"SECONDS\"` | `TimeUnit` (any enum) | Enum name, case-sensitive |\n| `\"0xF\"`, `\"017\"` | `int`/`long` (and boxed) | Hex and octal literals work |\n| `\"java.lang.Integer\"` | `Class<?>` | Fully-qualified name required |\n| `\"java.lang.Thread$State\"` | `Class<?>` | `$` for nested classes |\n| `\"byte\"` | `Class<?>` | Primitive type names work |\n| `\"char[]\"` | `Class<?>` | Array type names work |\n| `\"PT3S\"`, `\"PT1H30M\"` | `Duration` | ISO 8601 duration format |\n| `\"P2M6D\"` | `Period` | ISO 8601 period format |\n| `\"JPY\"` | `Currency` | ISO 4217 currency code |\n| `\"en-US\"` | `Locale` | IETF BCP 47 language tag |\n\n**Enums**: Write the enum constant name — `\"SECONDS\"` not `\"TimeUnit.SECONDS\"`. The parameter type tells JUnit which enum to use.\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario       | Unit    | Duration?\n    In seconds     | SECONDS | PT3S\n    In minutes     | MINUTES | PT5M\n    \"\"\")\nvoid formats_duration(TimeUnit unit, Duration duration) { ... }\n```\n\n---\n\n## Writing Custom Converter Methods\n\nWhen JUnit's built-in converters don't support your parameter type, add custom type converter methods annotated with `@TypeConverter`.\n\n## Java\n\nPlace custom converter methods as `@TypeConverter`-annotated `public static` methods in **a public test class** or a class listed in `@TypeConverterSources`.\n\n**IMPORTANT:** The test class must be declared `public` for TableTest to discover converter methods:\n```java\npublic class MyTest {  // Must be public, not package-private\n    // Converter methods here will be found\n}\n```\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Date       | Days Until?\n    today      | 0\n    tomorrow   | 1\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testDaysUntil(LocalDate date, int expected) {\n    assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static LocalDate parseLocalDate(String input) {\n    return switch (input) {\n        case \"today\" -> LocalDate.now();\n        case \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1);\n        default -> LocalDate.parse(input);\n    };\n}\n```\n\n## Kotlin\n\nFor Kotlin tests, there are two ways to declare converter methods:\n\n### Option 1: Package-Level Functions\n\nDeclare converter methods at package level in the same file as the test class:\n\n```kotlin\n// At package level (top of file, outside class)\n@TypeConverter\nfun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n    \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n    \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n    else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n}\n\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        Date       | Days Until?\n        today      | 0\n        tomorrow   | 1\n        \"\"\")\n    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n    }\n}\n```\n\n### Option 2: Companion Object with @JvmStatic\n\nDeclare converter methods in the companion object with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n\n```kotlin\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        Date       | Days Until?\n        today      | 0\n        tomorrow   | 1\n        \"\"\")\n    fun testDaysUntil(date: LocalDate, expected: Int) {\n        assertEquals(expected, ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(LocalDate.now(), date))\n    }\n\n    companion object {\n        @JvmStatic\n        @TypeConverter\n        fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n            \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n            \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n            else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n        }\n    }\n}\n```\n\n**Note**: `@Nested` inner classes in Kotlin cannot have companion objects. Use package-level functions or outer class companion object instead.\n\n## Using @TypeConverterSources\n\nFor shared converter methods across multiple test classes:\n\n**Java:**\n```java\n@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters.class)\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        ...\n        \"\"\")\n    void testWithSharedConverters(LocalDate date, Duration duration) { ... }\n}\n```\n\n**Kotlin** — use an `object` declaration with `@JvmStatic` and `@TypeConverter`:\n```kotlin\nobject DateConverters {\n    @JvmStatic\n    @TypeConverter\n    fun parseLocalDate(input: String): LocalDate = when (input) {\n        \"today\" -> LocalDate.now()\n        \"tomorrow\" -> LocalDate.now().plusDays(1)\n        else -> LocalDate.parse(input)\n    }\n}\n\n@TypeConverterSources(DateConverters::class)\nclass DateTest {\n    @TableTest(\"\"\"\n        ...\n        \"\"\")\n    fun testWithSharedConverters(date: LocalDate, duration: Duration) { ... }\n}\n```\n\n## Converter Method Requirements\n\nA converter method will be used when it:\n1. Is annotated with `@TypeConverter`\n2. Is defined as a `public static` method in a `public class`\n3. Accepts exactly one parameter\n4. Returns an object of the target parameter type\n5. **Is the only `@TypeConverter` method matching the above criteria in the class**\n\nThere is no specific naming pattern required, but `parse<TypeName>` (e.g., `parseLocalDate`, `parseMoney`) is conventional.\n\n### One Converter Method Per Target Type\n\nYou cannot have multiple converter methods with the same target type (same return type), even with different names:\n\n```java\n// WRONG - TableTest cannot determine which to use\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Map<String, String> parseRequest(String value) { ... }\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Map<String, String> parseResponse(String value) { ... }\n\n// CORRECT - Single converter handles both formats\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Map<String, String> parseRequestOrResponse(String value) {\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        // Handle response format\n    } else {\n        // Handle request format\n    }\n    return ...;\n}\n```\n\n**Even better - split into separate columns instead:**\n```java\n// BEST - No converter method needed\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Request Status | Request ms | Response Status? | Response ms?\n    OK             | 10         | OK               | <50\n    \"\"\")\nvoid test(String reqStatus, Integer reqMs, String respStatus, Long respMs) {\n    // Use parseResponseTime converter for respMs to handle \"<50\" format\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n    }\n    return Long.parseLong(value);\n}\n```\n\n## Search Strategy\n\nTableTest searches for converter methods in this order (stops at first match):\n\n**Java:**\n1. Current test class (including inherited methods)\n2. Enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests, starting with direct outer class)\n3. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n4. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n\n**Kotlin:**\n1. Current file (package-level functions and outer class companion object)\n2. Classes listed in `@TypeConverterSources` (in order listed)\n3. `@TypeConverterSources` of enclosing classes (for `@Nested` tests)\n\nIf a converter method isn't being found, check that it meets the requirements above and is in a location that matches the search order.\n\n## Handling Null Values\n\nWhen table cells can be blank (representing null), use appropriate return types:\n\n### Primitive vs Boxed Types\n\n```java\n// WRONG - primitives cannot be null\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Value | Time\n    OK    | 10\n          |        <- Blank cell causes error: \"null cannot be assigned to primitive type long\"\n    \"\"\")\nvoid test(String value, long time) { ... }\n\n// CORRECT - use boxed type\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Value | Time\n    OK    | 10\n          |        <- Blank cell becomes null\n    \"\"\")\nvoid test(String value, Long time) { ... }\n```\n\n### Converter Methods with Null Input\n\nConverter methods receive `null` for blank cells. Handle this appropriately:\n\n```java\n// Return null for blank input\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n        return null;  // Blank cell -> null value\n    }\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n    }\n    return Long.parseLong(value);\n}\n\n// Return empty collection for blank input\n@TypeConverter\npublic static List<String> parseItems(String value) {\n    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n        return List.of();  // Blank cell -> empty list\n    }\n    return Arrays.asList(value.split(\",\"));\n}\n```\n\n**Common pattern:** Blank cells in the table map to null/empty in the domain.\n\n## Domain-Specific Formatting\n\nConverter methods enable domain-specific formatting conventions that improve table readability:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario     | Response Time?\n    Fast         | <50\n    Acceptable   | <150\n    Slow         | <500\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testResponseTime(Long maxResponseTimeMs) {\n    // maxResponseTimeMs is 50, 150, 500 respectively\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Long parseResponseTime(String value) {\n    if (value == null || value.isBlank()) {\n        return null;\n    }\n    // Handle \"less than\" notation\n    if (value.startsWith(\"<\")) {\n        return Long.valueOf(value.substring(1));\n    }\n    return Long.parseLong(value);\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- `<50` clearly communicates \"less than 50ms\" in the table\n- Converter handles the formatting detail\n- Table is self-documenting without code comments\n\n**Other examples:**\n- Duration: `5m`, `30s` -> milliseconds\n- Currency: `$100`, `EUR50` -> numeric values\n- Percentage: `50%` -> 0.5\n- File size: `10KB`, `5MB` -> bytes\n\n## Converter Methods with Defaults\n\nConverter methods can provide sensible defaults for missing map entries, making tables clearer when properties vary by scenario:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario          | Config                                  | Expected?\n    Basic request     | [method: GET]                           | OK\n    With timeout      | [method: POST, timeout: 5000]           | OK\n    Full config       | [method: POST, timeout: 5000, retry: 3] | OK\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testRequest(Map<String, String> config, String expected) {\n    RequestConfig request = buildRequestConfig(config);\n    assertEquals(expected, process(request));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static RequestConfig buildRequestConfig(Map<String, String> config) {\n    if (config == null || config.isEmpty()) {\n        return RequestConfig.defaults();\n    }\n    return new RequestConfig(\n        config.getOrDefault(\"method\", \"GET\"),           // Default: GET\n        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"timeout\", \"3000\")), // Default: 3000ms\n        config.get(\"auth\"),                             // Default: null (no auth)\n        parseInt(config.getOrDefault(\"retry\", \"1\")),     // Default: 1 retry\n        parseBoolean(config.getOrDefault(\"cache\", \"false\")) // Default: no cache\n    );\n}\n```\n\n**Benefits:**\n- Each scenario only specifies what varies from defaults\n- Table focuses on the interesting variations\n- Defaults are documented in one place (converter method)\n- No sea of blank columns\n\n**When to use:**\n- Different scenarios need different subsets of properties\n- Most properties have sensible default values\n- Alternative is many blank cells in separate columns\n\n**When not to use:**\n- All scenarios use the same properties (use separate columns instead)\n- No sensible defaults exist (every property is required)\n\n## Common Wrapper Types\n\nJUnit's built-in converters don't handle wrapper types like `Optional`, `Result`, or `Either`. Converter methods are needed for these common patterns.\n\n### Optional<T>\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario        | Feature Toggles      | Result?\n    Feature enabled | [feature-x: true]    | true\n    Not registered  | [:]                  | empty\n    Feature disabled| [feature-x: false]   | false\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testFeatureToggle(Map<String, Boolean> toggles, Optional<Boolean> result) {\n    assertEquals(result, featureResolver.find(\"feature-x\", toggles));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<Boolean> result(String value) {\n    return switch (value) {\n        case \"empty\" -> Optional.empty();\n        case \"true\" -> Optional.of(true);\n        case \"false\" -> Optional.of(false);\n        default -> throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n    };\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern**: Use simple string representations (`empty`, `true`, `false`) rather than trying to parse `Optional.empty()` syntax.\n\n### Optional<String>\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario     | Input  | Result?\n    Found        | active | active\n    Not found    | unused | empty\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testLookup(String input, Optional<String> result) {\n    assertEquals(result, lookup(input));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<String> result(String value) {\n    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n}\n```\n\n### Result<T, E> (or Either<L, R>)\n\nFor Result/Either types, use string notation to indicate success vs failure:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario       | Input | Result?\n    Valid input    | 42    | OK\n    Invalid input  | abc   | ERROR: Not a number\n    Out of range   | -1    | ERROR: Must be positive\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testValidation(String input, Result<Integer, String> result) {\n    assertEquals(result, validator.validate(input));\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Result<Integer, String> result(String value) {\n    if (value.equals(\"OK\")) {\n        return Result.ok(42);  // Use value from Input column\n    }\n    if (value.startsWith(\"ERROR: \")) {\n        return Result.error(value.substring(7));\n    }\n    throw new IllegalArgumentException(\"Unknown result: \" + value);\n}\n```\n\n**Pattern**: Prefix error cases with `ERROR:` to distinguish from success cases.\n\n**Better approach**: Use separate columns for success value and error message:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario       | Input | Success? | Error?\n    Valid input    | 42    | 42       |\n    Invalid input  | abc   |          | Not a number\n    Out of range   | -1    |          | Must be positive\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testValidation(String input, Integer success, String error) {\n    Result<Integer, String> expected = success != null\n        ? Result.ok(success)\n        : Result.error(error);\n    assertEquals(expected, validator.validate(input));\n}\n```\n\nThis avoids the converter method entirely and makes the table clearer.\n\n### Multiple Optional Parameters\n\nWhen you have multiple `Optional` parameters in the same test, use different column naming:\n\n```java\n@TableTest(\"\"\"\n    Scenario          | Primary?  | Fallback?\n    Both present      | primary   | fallback\n    Primary only      | primary   | empty\n    Fallback only     | empty     | fallback\n    Neither present   | empty     | empty\n    \"\"\")\nvoid testOptionals(Optional<String> primary, Optional<String> fallback) {\n    // Test with both optionals\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<String> primary(String value) {\n    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n}\n\n@TypeConverter\npublic static Optional<String> fallback(String value) {\n    return \"empty\".equals(value) ? Optional.empty() : Optional.of(value);\n}\n```\n\n**Important**: Column name matching works here. The converter method name should match the parameter name, and TableTest will use the appropriate converter for each column.\n","numLines":512,"startLine":1,"totalLines":512}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KREz5vrCfry34VRtyexqEu","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→# Dependency and Import Setup\n     2→\n     3→Prefer the version already used in the project. If adding a new dependency, check Maven Central and replace `VERSION`.\n     4→\n     5→## Maven\n     6→\n     7→```xml\n     8→<dependency>\n     9→    <groupId>org.tabletest</groupId>\n    10→    <artifactId>tabletest-junit</artifactId>\n    11→    <version>VERSION</version>\n    12→    <scope>test</scope>\n    13→</dependency>\n    14→```\n    15→\n    16→## Gradle\n    17→\n    18→```groovy\n    19→testImplementation \"org.tabletest:tabletest-junit:VERSION\"\n    20→```\n    21→\n    22→## Imports\n    23→\n    24→```java\n    25→import org.tabletest.junit.TableTest;\n    26→```\n    27→\n    28→Import `@Scenario` only when binding the scenario column:\n    29→```java\n    30→import org.tabletest.junit.Scenario;\n    31→```\n    32→\n    33→Import `@TypeConverter` for custom converter methods:\n    34→```java\n    35→import org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverter;\n    36→```\n    37→\n    38→Import `@TypeConverterSources` for shared converter sources:\n    39→```java\n    40→import org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverterSources;\n    41→```\n    42→\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"a4c3bb6d-ab68-4398-8b75-0c630070e2f4","uuid":"a404facd-7fd2-4549-8eb3-8f3a9673c176","timestamp":"2026-04-06T21:01:30.333Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/var/folders/fz/zxx_qbvj49gbql83cc5l47pr0000gn/T/eval-skill-1775508642173/skills/tabletest/references/dependency-setup.md","content":"# Dependency and Import Setup\n\nPrefer the version already used in the project. If adding a new dependency, check Maven Central and replace `VERSION`.\n\n## Maven\n\n```xml\n<dependency>\n    <groupId>org.tabletest</groupId>\n    <artifactId>tabletest-junit</artifactId>\n    <version>VERSION</version>\n    <scope>test</scope>\n</dependency>\n```\n\n## Gradle\n\n```groovy\ntestImplementation \"org.tabletest:tabletest-junit:VERSION\"\n```\n\n## Imports\n\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.TableTest;\n```\n\nImport `@Scenario` only when binding the scenario column:\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.Scenario;\n```\n\nImport `@TypeConverter` for custom converter methods:\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverter;\n```\n\nImport `@TypeConverterSources` for shared converter sources:\n```java\nimport org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverterSources;\n```\n","numLines":42,"startLine":1,"totalLines":42}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01WcP5hUQCJzJ24hWxF72Y3K","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the information I need. Let me write the complete Java test class.\n\nKey findings from the reference:\n1. `@TypeConverter` must be `public static` on a `public class`\n2. One converter per return type\n3. Import: `org.tabletest.junit.TableTest`, `org.tabletest.junit.TypeConverter`\n4. Handle null for blank cells in TypeConverter\n5. BigDecimal isn't built-in — need TypeConverter\n\nAlso from the type converter reference, I see an interesting pattern for result types — using separate columns for success/error is actually recommended:\n\n\nUsing separate columns for success value and error message aligns well with having `Success?` and `Message?` columns for CartResult and CheckoutResult.\n\nNow I need to figure out the TypeConverter strategy: I have `Cart` as both input and output, which one TypeConverter handles fine. For `BigDecimal`, I need a single TypeConverter to handle it as the expected value in the `Total?` column. I'm wondering if JUnit has built-in support for BigDecimal conversion through StringToNumberConverter, but I should probably write my own to be safe. I need to be careful about the domain model assumptions—particularly that Cart has a `getItems()` method and whether it's mutable or immutable, since the TypeConverter will be building Cart instances from strings. Let me document these assumptions clearly in the test description, then write out the complete Java code. I'm also reconsidering the table design for the coupon column—whether it should just be a plain string code or something more structured. I'm verifying the test data makes sense across all scenarios—when a coupon is expired, the cart should keep the old code; when replacing one coupon with another, it should update to the new code; and when there's no initial coupon, the result should reflect whatever